          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              A delta lens
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f, \varphi) : A \to B
              is equivalent to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon A \to B
              and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofunctor"} cofunctor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↛
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi \colon A \nrightarrow B
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi φ
                      %mn 0
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f a = \varphi_{0} a
              for all objects
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %msub
                        %mi φ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi fa
                      %mo →
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \varphi_{1}(a \in A, u \colon fa \to b \in B) = u
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              See (
              %a{:href => "#AhmanUustalu17"}> Ahman-Uustalu 2017, Section 6
              ) and (
              %a{:href => "#Clarke20a"}> Clarke 2020
              ).
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A delta lens between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indiscrete+category"} codiscrete categories
                is precisely a classical
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lens+%28in+computer+science%29"} lens (in computer science)
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#JohnsonRosebrugh16"} Johnson-Rosebrugh 2016, Proposition 4
            %li
              %p
                Every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
                yields a delta lens
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi disc
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi disc
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} disc(A) \rightarrow disc(B)
                between
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete categories
            %li
              %p
                Every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+fibration"} discrete opfibration
                is a delta lens whose lifting operation is determined by the unique
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+morphism"} opcartesian lifts
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi f
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi w
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(a, f w) = w
                holds for all morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi w
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi a
                      %mo →
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w \colon a \to a'
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %p
                A split
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} Grothendieck opfibration
                is a delta lens whose chosen lifts are
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+morphism"} opcartesian morphisms
            %li
              %p
                Dually, a split
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} Grothendieck fibration
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
                is a delta lens
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mi op
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mi B
                          %mi op
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{op} \to B^{op}
            %li
              %p
                Every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+epimorphism"} split epimorphism
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoids
                with a chosen
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
                is a delta lens, when the monoids a considered as categories with a single object.
            %li
              %p
                More generally, every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijective+on+objects+functor"} bijective on objects functor
                with a chosen
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
                is a delta lens.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lens+%28in+computer+science%29"} lens (in computer science)
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The notion of delta lens was first defined in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+science"} computer science
            as a generalization of the classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lens+%28in+computer+science%29"} lenses
            between sets:
          %ul
            %li#DiskinXiongCzarnecki11
              Zinovy Diskin, Yingfei Xiong, Krzysztof Czarnecki,
              = succeed "," do
                %em From State- to Delta-Based Bidirectional Model Transformations: the Asymmetric Case
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.5381/jot.2011.10.1.a6"} doi:10.5381/jot.2011.10.1.a6
          %p
            The connection between delta lenses and split
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} Grothendieck opfibrations
            was first explored in the paper:
          %ul
            %li#JohnsonRosebrugh13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Johnson"}> Michael Johnson
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Rosebrugh"} Robert Rosebrugh
              = succeed "," do
                %em Delta Lenses and Opfibrations
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.14279/tuj.eceasst.57.875"} doi:10.14279/tuj.eceasst.57.875
          %p
            The following paper details the connection between delta lenses and the classical
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lens+%28in+computer+science%29"} lenses
          %ul
            %li#JohnsonRosebrugh16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Johnson"}> Michael Johnson
              ,
              = succeed "," do
